          'content': 'WHAT IS CODING-DECODING?\nA coding system hides the real meaning of a message by substituting letters, numbers, or symbols according to a fixed rule. The task is to FIND the rule and apply it.\n\nTYPE 1 — LETTER SHIFT CODING\nEach letter is replaced by another letter at a fixed distance in the alphabet.\n\nForward Shift (+):\nIf A=B, B=C, C=D (shift +1)\nCAT = DBU\n\nBackward Shift (-):\nIf A=Z, B=A, C=B (shift -1)\nCAT = BZS\n\nShift +2: A=C, B=D, C=E, D=F\nShift +3: A=D, B=E (Caesar cipher)\n\nHOW TO FIND SHIFT:\nStep 1: Take first letter of coded word\nStep 2: Find difference from original letter\nStep 3: Apply same difference to all letters\n\nExample: If COME = FRPH\nC→F = +3, O→R = +3, M→P = +3, E→H = +3\nShift is +3\nSo DONE = G(+3)R(+3)Q(+3)H(+3) = GRQH\n\nTYPE 2 — REVERSE ALPHABET CODING\nA↔Z, B↔Y, C↔X, D↔W, E↔V, F↔U\nG↔T, H↔S, I↔R, J↔Q, K↔P, L↔O, M↔N\nMemory tip: A+Z=27, B+Y=27, all pairs sum to 27\n\nExample: CAT in reverse coding\nC(3)→X(24), A(1)→Z(26), T(20)→G(7)\nCAT = XZG\n\nTYPE 3 — NUMBER CODING\nStandard: A=1, B=2, C=3 ... Z=26\nReverse: A=26, B=25, C=24 ... Z=1\n\nExample (standard): DOOR = 4+15+15+18 = 52\nExample: If position-based: FACE = 6+1+3+5 = 15\n\nTYPE 4 — SKIP LETTER CODING\nAlternate letters taken, or letters skipped.\nExample: ACEGI (every alternate letter)\n\nTYPE 5 — SYMBOL CODING\nLetters replaced by symbols. Just map the symbols.\nExample: If A=@, B=*, C=# then CAB = #@*\n\nTYPE 6 — MIXED CODING (SENTENCE CODING)\nThree sentences given with some common words and their codes mixed. Find code for a specific word.\n\nMethod:\n1. Find two sentences with one common word\n2. The code that appears in both = code for common word\n3. Eliminate known codes to find remaining ones\n\nExample:\n\'sky is blue\' = \'pit na ja\'\n\'grass is green\' = \'sit na ho\'\nCommon word: \'is\', Common code: \'na\'\nSo \'is\' = \'na\'\n\nEXAM TIPS:\n1. Always write the alphabet with numbers 1-26 on rough paper\n2. Check if shift is same for ALL letters before concluding\n3. In sentence coding, find the word that appears in maximum sentences\n4. Time: 45-60 seconds per question',
