          'id': 'profit-loss',
          'title': 'Profit & Loss',
          'weightage': '3-4 questions',
          'difficulty': 'Easy-Medium',
          'readTime': '15 mins',
          'content': 'FUNDAMENTAL CONCEPTS:\n• Cost Price (CP): Price at which article is BOUGHT\n• Selling Price (SP): Price at which article is SOLD\n• Marked Price (MP): Price written on the tag / listed price\n• Discount: Reduction given on Marked Price\n\nPROFIT/LOSS FORMULAS:\nProfit = SP - CP (when SP > CP)\nLoss = CP - SP (when CP > SP)\nProfit% = (Profit/CP) × 100\nLoss% = (Loss/CP) × 100\n\n⚠️ IMPORTANT: Profit% and Loss% are ALWAYS calculated on CP, not SP\n\nFINDING SP:\nSP = CP × (100 + Profit%)/100 [for profit]\nSP = CP × (100 - Loss%)/100 [for loss]\n\nFINDING CP:\nCP = SP × 100/(100 + Profit%) [given SP and profit%]\nCP = SP × 100/(100 - Loss%) [given SP and loss%]\n\nDISCOUNT FORMULAS:\n⚠️ Discount is ALWAYS calculated on MP (not CP)\nDiscount = MP - SP\nDiscount% = (Discount/MP) × 100\nSP = MP × (100 - Discount%)/100\n\nCOMBINED: Finding CP given MP and discount and profit:\nSP = MP × (100-D%)/100\nCP = SP × 100/(100+P%)\n\nFALSE WEIGHT TRICK:\nIf trader claims to sell at CP but uses faulty weight:\nActual weight given = w grams (instead of 1000g)\nProfit% = (1000-w)/w × 100\n\nTWO ARTICLES AT SAME SP:\nIf two articles sold at same SP, one at x% profit, one at x% loss:\nALWAYS a net LOSS\nLoss% = x²/100\nExample: Each sold at ₹100, one at 20% profit, one at 20% loss\nLoss% = 20²/100 = 4%\n\nEQUIVALENT DISCOUNT:\nTwo successive discounts d1% and d2%:\nEquivalent single discount = d1 + d2 - (d1×d2)/100\n\nExample: 20% and 10% successive discounts\n= 20 + 10 - (20×10)/100 = 30 - 2 = 28% equivalent\n\nMARKUP AND DISCOUNT:\nIf article marked up by x% and discount of y% given:\nProfit/Loss% = x - y - xy/100\n\nCOST + OVERHEAD:\nIf overhead expenses are z% of CP:\nEffective CP = CP × (100+z)/100\n\nCOMMON TRICKS IN EXAM:\n1. \'Gain of 25 paise in rupee\' means gain of 25%\n2. \'Loss of 1/4 of CP\' means loss of 25%\n3. If SP = 4/5 of CP → loss = 1/5 of CP = 20% loss\n4. If SP = 5/4 of CP → profit = 1/4 of CP = 25% profit',
