          'content': 'SIMPLE INTEREST:\nSI = (P × R × T)/100\nAmount (A) = P + SI = P(1 + RT/100)\nP = Principal, R = Rate per annum, T = Time in years\n\nCompound Interest (CI):\nA = P(1 + R/100)^T\nCI = A - P = P[(1 + R/100)^T - 1]\n\nDIFFERENT COMPOUNDING PERIODS:\nHalf-yearly: A = P(1 + R/200)^(2T)\n[Rate halved, time doubled]\n\nQuarterly: A = P(1 + R/400)^(4T)\n[Rate quartered, time quadrupled]\n\nMonthly: A = P(1 + R/1200)^(12T)\n\nSI vs CI COMPARISON:\nFor same P, R, T: CI > SI always (except T=1 year where CI = SI)\nFor T=1 year: CI = SI = PR/100\nFor T=2 years: CI - SI = P(R/100)² = PR²/10000\nFor T=3 years: CI - SI = P(R/100)²(3 + R/100)\n\nExample: P=10000, R=10%, T=2 years\nSI = 10000×10×2/100 = ₹2000\nCI = 10000(1.1)² - 10000 = 12100 - 10000 = ₹2100\nCI - SI = 2100 - 2000 = ₹100\nUsing formula: 10000 × (10/100)² = 10000 × 0.01 = ₹100 ✓\n\nUSEFUL SHORTCUTS:\nRule of 72: Money doubles in ≈ 72/R years (at CI)\nExample: At 8% CI, money doubles in 72/8 = 9 years\n\nEffective Rate for half-yearly compounding at R%:\nEffective annual rate = (1+R/200)² - 1\n\nPRACTICAL EXAMPLES:\n1. P=5000, R=8%, T=3 years (SI)\n   SI = 5000×8×3/100 = ₹1200\n   Amount = 5000+1200 = ₹6200\n\n2. P=8000, R=10%, T=2 years (CI half-yearly)\n   A = 8000(1+10/200)^4 = 8000(1.05)^4\n   = 8000 × 1.2155 = ₹9724\n\nFINDING RATE/TIME WHEN OTHER VALUES KNOWN:\nFrom SI formula: R = SI×100/(P×T)\nFrom CI: Use logarithms or try values\n\nSUM BECOMES DOUBLE: N = 100/R (simple interest)\nSUM BECOMES TRIPLE: N = 200/R (simple interest)',
